    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Accessing String Characters"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "You can access characters in a string using bracket notation ([ ]) along with their index. For example,"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "content": "1let word = \"Hello\";\n2\n3console.log(word[0]);    // Output: H\n4\n5console.log(word[10]);    // Output: undefined"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "When using [ ], if the character doesn't exist, the output is undefined."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "The charAt() Method"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "You can also access characters in a string using the charAt() method. For example,"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "content": "1let word = \"Hello\";\n2\n3console.log(word.charAt(1));    // Output: e\n4\n5console.log(word[10]);    // Output:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "When using charAt(), if the character doesn't exist, the output is an empty string."
    },
    {
